Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Slab chunks and cache objects as Sublet child lifetimes (CDERIVE/CREVOKE)
Upstream: memcached 1.6.45
Upstream-Archive: https://memcached.org/files/memcached-1.6.45.tar.gz
Upstream-SHA256: d362c64e6d8d5287153501eabf7c85b4a761432fbf53f5d7b085d0bb1653c1dd
Build-Variants: only with -DMC_CAPSTONE_SUBLET, on the virtual profile (musl mallocng), with
  -I capstone/runtime/include (capability.h) and NDEBUG; every other build is unchanged
Depends-On: memcached-1.6.45-0001-capability-alignment.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

memcached's slab allocator never frees a chunk: do_slabs_free files it on its class's free list
and do_slabs_alloc hands it out again, and cache.c keeps freed objects on a list the same way. A
stale item or buffer pointer therefore reaches whatever the chunk holds next. Two instructions,
inserted where the allocators hand memory out and take it back, end that
(docs/design/virtual-capstone/isa.md): CDERIVE makes a child capability of a non-linear parent,
bounded to a sub-range and carrying MANAGE over its own children; CREVOKE(parent, child) ends a
direct child and everything below it, and faults when the child is not a live direct child.

Slabs. Each page carries two lifetimes:
- memory_allocate derives the page's lifetime from the memory it got; the slab lists hold it.
- split_slab_page_into_freelist derives the generation for the class the page now serves, and
  writes it into every chunk header (item.sublet_parent; item_chunk has the same slot, so the two
  layouts stay in step and every item grows by one capability).
- do_slabs_alloc returns a child of that generation bounded to the class's chunk size.
- slabs_free reads the generation through the caller's own pointer, revokes the chunk and passes
  do_slabs_free a pointer at the same address derived from the generation. The chained chunks of a
  large item are revoked the same way in do_slabs_free_chunked.
- slabs_finalize_page_move revokes the page's generation when the page leaves its class. That
  ends, in one instruction, every chunk the page handed out, including the ones the mover rescued
  or reclaimed in place without a free.

Chunks are bounded to the chunk size and not to the request: slabs_alloc is not told the request,
and the mover copies whole chunks (memcpy of cls_size into a chunk it allocated).

The mover walks a page through the page's own pointer, which is not a child of the generation, so
it cannot be revoked. For a linked item (MOVE_FROM_LRU, MOVE_BUSY_ACTIVE) the mover therefore takes
the hash table's reference to it under the item lock, and every unlink, replace or remove that may
free it uses that reference. Its three whole-chunk copies keep the destination's own parent.

Cache. cache_create reserves one capability in front of each object. A new object's lifetime is
derived from the memory malloc returned and kept in that slot; cache_alloc returns a child of it
covering the slot and the object, positioned at the object; cache_free reads the lifetime from the
slot, revokes the caller's object and files the lifetime on the free list. Freeing the object, or
a page, frees the derived lifetime: the virtual heap's free() validates by a load and finds the
slot by address (runtime/virtual/heap.c checked()). The debug redzone would share the slot, so
the build requires NDEBUG, which memcached's own Makefile sets.

What it gives up: a live pointer can read the slot in front of it, as it could read the item header
without the patch, and so can load the generation or the object's lifetime, which have page-wide
or object-wide bounds and MANAGE. A stale pointer cannot, since it is revoked. Overwriting the slot
clears its tag and the next free faults. Restart mode (-e) rebuilds item pointers from addresses,
so the items it restores are not children of a generation and their first free faults.

